{"name":"Pergola Panorama","hex":"#e1e9db","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pergola","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A framework in the form of a passageway of columns that supports a trelliswork roof; used to support and train climbing plants.","Such a framework employed to provide shade, especially over a patio."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pergola"},{"term":"panorama","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An unbroken view of an entire surrounding area.","A picture or series of pictures representing a continuous scene."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/panorama"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pergola","blocks":[{"text":"A pergola is most commonly used as an outdoor garden feature forming a shaded walkway, passageway, or sitting area of vertical posts or pillars that usually support crossbeams and a sturdy open lattice, often upon which woody vines are trained. The origin of the word is the Late Latin pergula, referring to a projecting eave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Rose_Pergola_at_Kew_Gardens.jpg/330px-Rose_Pergola_at_Kew_Gardens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergola","wikidata":"Q264458"},{"title":"Panorama","blocks":[{"text":"A panorama is any wide-angle view or representation of a physical space, whether in painting, drawing, photography, film, seismic images, or 3D modeling. The word was coined in the 18th century by the English painter Robert Barker to describe his panoramic paintings of Edinburgh and London. The motion-picture term panning is derived from panorama."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/360%C2%B0_interior_panorama_of_San_Gregorio_church_in_Breglia%2C_Italy.jpg/330px-360%C2%B0_interior_panorama_of_San_Gregorio_church_in_Breglia%2C_Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"